(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-appendicular-musculature-quiz"]={"title":"The Appendicular Musculature — Anatomy Exam 4","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"How is the skeletal musculature of the body subdivided, per the material?","choices":["Axial musculature and appendicular musculature","Voluntary musculature and involuntary musculature","Superficial musculature and deep musculature","Flexor musculature and extensor musculature"],"answer":0,"exp":"The skeletal muscle of the body is subdivided into axial musculature (positions the head/vertebral column and moves the rib cage) and appendicular musculature (stabilizes/moves the appendicular skeleton). — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which of the following is a listed function of the appendicular muscles?","choices":["Housing red bone marrow within the medullary cavity","Absorbing shocks and jolts as you walk, run, or jump","Moving the rib cage during quiet and forced respiration","Positioning the head and vertebral column upright"],"answer":1,"exp":"Appendicular muscles stabilize the pectoral and pelvic girdles, move the upper and lower limbs, absorb shocks and jolts during walking/running/jumping, and aid in strengthening the joint area. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What are the two major groups of appendicular muscles?","choices":["Intrinsic muscles confined to the hand and foot; extrinsic muscles confined to the forearm and leg","Muscles of the pectoral girdle and upper limbs; muscles of the pelvic girdle and lower limbs","Muscles that produce flexion at each joint; muscles that produce extension at each joint","Superficial muscles lying near the skin surface; deep muscles lying close to the bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"The two major groups of appendicular muscles are the muscles of the pectoral girdle and upper limbs, and the muscles of the pelvic girdle and lower limbs. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which muscle elevates, depresses, and rotates the scapula and/or clavicle, and can also extend the neck?","choices":["Serratus anterior","Trapezius","Rhomboid major","Levator scapulae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The trapezius can elevate, depress, and rotate the scapula and/or clavicle, and can also extend the neck. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which nerve is associated with the serratus anterior, and what condition results from its dysfunction?","choices":["Radial nerve; dysfunction classically causes wrist drop","Axillary nerve; dysfunction causes deltoid muscle paralysis","Musculocutaneous nerve; dysfunction causes biceps brachii paralysis","Long thoracic nerve; dysfunction causes serratus anterior paralysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The serratus anterior is innervated by the long thoracic nerve; a lesion of this nerve causes serratus anterior paralysis. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which muscles both adduct and produce downward rotation of the scapula?","choices":["Rhomboid major and rhomboid minor","Pectoralis minor and subclavius","Serratus anterior and subclavius","Trapezius and levator scapulae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Both the rhomboid major and rhomboid minor adduct the scapula and produce downward rotation of the scapula. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which four rotator cuff muscles hold the humerus in position?","choices":["Teres major, latissimus dorsi, triceps brachii, and deltoid","Deltoid, pectoralis major, coracobrachialis, and biceps brachii","Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres major, and deltoid","Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The rotator cuff muscles are the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis, which together hold the humerus in position. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which muscle abducts the arm at the shoulder?","choices":["Deltoid","Latissimus dorsi","Pectoralis major","Coracobrachialis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The deltoid abducts the arm at the shoulder. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which muscle produces flexion, adduction, and medial rotation of the arm at the shoulder?","choices":["Deltoid muscle","Infraspinatus muscle","Teres minor muscle","Pectoralis major"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pectoralis major produces flexion, adduction, and medial rotation of the arm at the shoulder. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which muscle produces extension, adduction, and medial rotation at the shoulder, and is a major muscle of the posterior thoraco-lumbar region?","choices":["Coracobrachialis","Supraspinatus","Subscapularis","Latissimus dorsi"],"answer":3,"exp":"Latissimus dorsi produces extension, adduction, and medial rotation at the shoulder. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which two muscles are exceptions to the general rule that forearm/hand movers originate on the humerus, because they instead originate on the scapula?","choices":["Pronator teres and the supinator, both forearm rotators","Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris, both wrist flexors","Brachialis and brachioradialis, both elbow flexors","Long head of triceps brachii and long head of biceps brachii"],"answer":3,"exp":"The long head of triceps brachii (inserting on the olecranon) and the long head of biceps brachii (inserting on the radial tuberosity) are exceptions, originating on the scapula rather than the humerus. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which muscle flexes the elbow and is one of the primary muscles for that action, alongside brachialis and brachioradialis?","choices":["Supinator","Triceps brachii","Biceps brachii","Anconeus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Biceps brachii flexes at the elbow (and also at the shoulder), alongside brachialis and brachioradialis, which also flex at the elbow. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Where do all three heads of the triceps brachii insert?","choices":["The coracoid process of the scapula","The olecranon of the elbow","The radial tuberosity","The medial epicondyle of the humerus"],"answer":1,"exp":"All three heads of the triceps brachii (lateral, long, and medial) insert on the olecranon of the elbow. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which muscle pronates the forearm and is located deep, spanning between the distal ulna and radius?","choices":["Supinator muscle","Brachioradialis muscle","Pronator quadratus","Pronator teres muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pronator quadratus pronates the forearm and is a deep muscle spanning between the distal ulna and radius. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which muscle flexes and abducts the wrist, named for its relationship to the radius?","choices":["Palmaris longus","Flexor carpi radialis","Extensor carpi radialis longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris"],"answer":1,"exp":"Flexor carpi radialis flexes and abducts the wrist. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which muscle flexes and adducts the wrist, on the ulnar side of the forearm?","choices":["Flexor carpi ulnaris","Palmaris longus","Flexor carpi radialis","Extensor carpi ulnaris"],"answer":0,"exp":"Flexor carpi ulnaris flexes and adducts the wrist. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which three extensor muscles act on the wrist, producing extension and either abduction or adduction?","choices":["Flexor carpi radialis, flexor carpi ulnaris, and palmaris longus, the primary wrist flexor group","Extensor carpi radialis longus, extensor carpi radialis brevis, and extensor carpi ulnaris","Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, and extensor pollicis longus, acting on the thumb","Extensor digitorum, extensor indicis, and extensor digiti minimi, acting primarily on the fingers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis extend and abduct the wrist, while extensor carpi ulnaris extends and adducts the wrist — together the three named wrist extensors. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What distinguishes extrinsic muscles of the hand from intrinsic muscles of the hand?","choices":["There is no functional or locational difference between extrinsic and intrinsic hand muscles; both groups perform identical roles in grip strength and dexterity","Extrinsic muscles are found only within the palm and dorsum of the hand; intrinsic muscles are found only in the forearm, reversing the usual pattern","Extrinsic muscles provide fine motor control for delicate finger movements; intrinsic muscles provide only gross motor control and crude grip strength","Extrinsic muscles are found mostly in the lower arm and provide strength/crude control; intrinsic muscles are found in the palm/dorsum and provide fine control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Extrinsic muscles of the hand are relatively large muscles found mostly in the lower arm that provide strength and crude control of the hand and fingers, while intrinsic muscles are smaller muscles found mostly in the palm or dorsum of the hand that provide fine control. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which muscle produces abduction of the thumb and is noted with an asterisk as a key muscle in the material?","choices":["Extensor indicis","Extensor pollicis brevis","Extensor digiti minimi","Abductor pollicis longus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Abductor pollicis longus produces abduction of the thumb. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which muscle flexes the thumb specifically, distinguishing it from the flexor digitorum muscles that act on the other digits?","choices":["Flexor digitorum profundus","Extensor pollicis brevis","Flexor pollicis longus","Flexor digitorum superficialis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Flexor pollicis longus produces flexion of the thumb. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which intrinsic hand muscle brings the thumb into opposition, a key movement for grasping?","choices":["Flexor pollicis brevis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Opponens pollicis","Adductor pollicis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Opponens pollicis produces opposition of the thumb. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which intrinsic hand muscles are responsible for flexion of the digits and are named for their unique, worm-like shape?","choices":["Palmaris brevis","Palmar interossei","Lumbricals","Dorsal interossei"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lumbricals are responsible for flexion of the digits. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"How do the dorsal interossei and palmar interossei differ in their actions on the fingers?","choices":["Dorsal interossei flex the digits; palmar interossei extend the digits","Dorsal interossei adduct digits 2-4; palmar interossei abduct digits 2, 4, and 5","Both abduct the same digits","Dorsal interossei abduct digits 2-4; palmar interossei adduct digits 2, 4, and 5"],"answer":3,"exp":"Dorsal interossei abduct digits 2 through 4, while palmar interossei adduct digits 2, 4, and 5. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which muscle tenses the skin on the ulnar side of the palm during grip action?","choices":["Opponens digiti minimi","Palmaris longus","Palmaris brevis","Abductor digiti minimi"],"answer":2,"exp":"Palmaris brevis tenses the skin on the ulnar side of the palm during grip action. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which peripheral nerve injury is specifically associated with paralysis of the serratus anterior in the material?","choices":["Radial nerve lesion","Median nerve lesion (carpal tunnel syndrome)","Long thoracic nerve lesion","Axillary nerve lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Serratus anterior paralysis is typically due to dysfunction (a lesion) of the long thoracic nerve. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which nerve compression syndrome affecting the wrist and hand is mentioned as a clinical correlation in the material?","choices":["Serratus anterior paralysis","Carpal tunnel syndrome","Whiplash","Boutonniere deformity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Carpal tunnel syndrome is mentioned as a clinical correlation affecting the wrist and hand, related to compression within the carpal tunnel. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the Boutonniere deformity, as referenced in the material's clinical correlation on hand muscles?","choices":["A wrist fracture pattern seen after a fall on an outstretched hand","A finger deformity noted alongside the intrinsic hand muscle content","A shoulder girdle nerve palsy from serratus anterior weakness","A hip joint dislocation following high-energy trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Boutonniere deformity is referenced as a clinical correlation associated with the finger/hand musculature and tendon anatomy covered in the material. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the \"action line\" of a muscle?","choices":["The path traced by the nerve that supplies the contracting muscle","The direction the muscle moves upon developing tension when it contracts","The line marking exactly where a muscle originates on the bone","The boundary separating the superficial from the deep muscle layers"],"answer":1,"exp":"When a muscle contracts, it develops tension, and the direction the muscle moves upon developing tension is known as the action line. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"According to the material, an action line crossing the medial aspect of a joint classifies a muscle as which type?","choices":["An extensor, crossing the posterior joint aspect","A flexor, crossing the anterior joint aspect","An abductor, crossing the lateral joint aspect","An adductor (sometimes called a medial rotator)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Action lines that cross the medial aspect of a joint classify a muscle as an adductor, sometimes called a medial rotator. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Action lines that cross the anterior aspect of a joint classify a muscle as which type?","choices":["An abductor/lateral rotator","An extensor","An adductor/medial rotator","A flexor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Action lines that cross the anterior aspect of a joint classify a muscle as a flexor. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
